LONG5.push(
 {t:'Sylvia Mendez and the Fight for Fair Schools',k:'Article',p:[
  'In 1944, eight-year-old Sylvia Mendez and her brothers tried to enroll at the 17th Street School in Westminster, California. The school turned them away. Officials told the family that the Mendez children had to attend a separate school for Mexican American students.',
  'The separate school was a small wooden building near a cow pasture. It had fewer books and supplies than the school down the street. Sylvia\'s parents, Gonzalo and Felícitas Mendez, believed this was unfair.',
  'In 1945, the Mendez family and four other families went to court. They argued that separating children because of their background broke the law. Many people in the community, and groups from across the country, supported them.',
  'In 1946, a federal judge agreed with the families. The school districts appealed, but in 1947 a higher court upheld the decision. That same year, California Governor Earl Warren signed a law ending school segregation in the state.',
  'The case helped prepare the way for Brown versus Board of Education, the 1954 Supreme Court decision that ended school segregation across the nation. By then, Earl Warren was the Chief Justice of the Supreme Court.',
  'Sylvia Mendez spent much of her adult life telling students about the case. In 2011, she received the Presidential Medal of Freedom, the highest honor a civilian in the United States can receive.'],
  q:[['mc','What is the main idea of the article?','The Mendez family\'s court case helped end school segregation in California and beyond.',['The 17th Street School was a large building.','Sylvia Mendez had several brothers.','Earl Warren became Chief Justice.'],'What is the whole article mostly about?','It explains how <b>the Mendez case helped end school segregation</b>.'],
     ['ab','<b>Part A:</b> Why did the Mendez family believe the separate school was unfair?','It had fewer resources than the other school.',['It was too far from the ocean.','It taught only in English.','It had too many teachers.'],'<b>Part B:</b> Which sentence supports your answer in Part A?','It had fewer books and supplies than the school down the street.',['In 1945, the Mendez family and four other families went to court.','In 2011, she received the Presidential Medal of Freedom, the highest honor a civilian in the United States can receive.','Sylvia Mendez spent much of her adult life telling students about the case.'],'Look at paragraph 2.','The separate school had <b>fewer books and supplies</b>.'],
     ['two','Which <b>two</b> events happened in 1947?',['A higher court upheld the decision.','Governor Earl Warren signed a law ending school segregation in California.'],['The Mendez children were turned away.','Brown versus Board of Education was decided.','Sylvia received the Medal of Freedom.'],'Find the paragraph with 1947.','In 1947, the appeal was decided and Warren <b>signed the law</b>.'],
     ['hl','Tap the sentence that explains how the Mendez case connects to the whole nation.','The case helped prepare the way for Brown versus Board of Education','Look at paragraph 5.','The case <b>prepared the way for Brown v. Board</b>.'],
     ['mc','How is the article mainly organized?','in time order (chronological)',['as a list of opinions','as a comparison of two schools only','as problem and solution with no dates'],'Look at the years.','The years 1944, 1945, 1946, 1947, 1954 and 2011 show <b>time order</b>.'],
     ['mc','In paragraph 4, what does <b>upheld</b> mean?','kept or supported',['threw out','lifted into the air','forgot about'],'The higher court agreed with the judge.','<b>Upheld</b> means the court kept the decision.'],
     ['hl','Tap the sentence that tells what Sylvia did as an adult.','Sylvia Mendez spent much of her adult life telling students about the case.','Look at the last paragraph.','She <b>taught students</b> about the case.']]},
 {t:'The Robot Who Learned to Dance',k:'Story',p:[
  'Unit 7 was built to sort packages. It could lift heavy boxes, scan labels in a blink, and never once dropped anything. But every evening, when the warehouse lights dimmed, Unit 7 watched the night guard, Grace, sway to music from her little radio.',
  '"What is that movement?" Unit 7 asked one night.',
  '"Dancing," Grace said, laughing. "You don\'t do it for any reason. You do it because it feels good."',
  'Unit 7 did not understand. Everything it did had a reason. Still, it began to practice. It rolled forward, then back. It spun in a careful circle. It knocked over a stack of boxes and beeped in alarm.',
  'For weeks, Unit 7 practiced after its shift. Grace showed it how to tap one wheel to the beat. The robot\'s steps were stiff, but they were getting better.',
  'One evening, the warehouse workers stayed late for a party. When the music started, everyone was too shy to dance. Then Unit 7 rolled to the middle of the floor and spun in a perfect circle. The workers cheered, and one by one, they joined in.',
  'Later, Grace patted the robot\'s metal arm. "So," she asked, "did you find a reason?"',
  'Unit 7\'s lights blinked softly. "Yes," it said. "Everyone else."'],
  q:[['mc','Which is the best theme of the story?','Trying something new can bring people together.',['Robots should only do their jobs.','Parties are a waste of time.','It is impossible to learn new skills.'],'What happened because Unit 7 learned to dance?','Unit 7\'s dancing <b>brought everyone together</b>.'],
     ['ab','<b>Part A:</b> How does Unit 7 change during the story?','It goes from doing things only for reasons to enjoying something for others.',['It goes from friendly to angry.','It forgets how to sort packages.','It stops talking to Grace.'],'<b>Part B:</b> Which sentence best supports your answer in Part A?','"Yes," it said. "Everyone else."',['It could lift heavy boxes, scan labels in a blink, and never once dropped anything.','It knocked over a stack of boxes and beeped in alarm.','Unit 7 was built to sort packages.'],'Look at the ending.','At the end, Unit 7 dances <b>for everyone else</b>.'],
     ['two','Which <b>two</b> details show that learning to dance was hard for Unit 7?',['It knocked over a stack of boxes and beeped in alarm.','The robot\'s steps were stiff, but they were getting better.'],['The workers cheered, and one by one, they joined in.','Unit 7 was built to sort packages.','Grace patted the robot\'s metal arm.'],'Look for mistakes and struggles.','Knocking over boxes and <b>stiff steps</b> show it was hard.'],
     ['hl','Tap the sentence that shows why the workers started dancing.','Then Unit 7 rolled to the middle of the floor and spun in a perfect circle.','Look at paragraph 6.','Unit 7 danced first, so the workers <b>felt brave enough</b> to join.'],
     ['mc','What does Grace mean when she says you dance "because it feels good"?','People dance for joy, not to get a job done.',['Dancing is good exercise only.','Dancing is a rule at work.','Only robots should dance.'],'Compare this with how Unit 7 thinks.','She means people dance for <b>joy</b>.'],
     ['mc','From whose point of view is the story told?','a narrator outside the story (third person)',['Unit 7, using "I"','Grace, using "I"','the warehouse workers, using "we"'],'Look for I or he/she/it.','A narrator uses "it" and "she": <b>third person</b>.'],
     ['hl','Tap the sentence that shows Grace helped Unit 7 learn.','Grace showed it how to tap one wheel to the beat.','Look at paragraph 5.','She <b>showed it how to tap one wheel</b>.']]},
 {t:'Should Our City Ban Plastic Bags? (two texts)',k:'Article',p:[
  '§Text 1: Ban the Bag',
  'Every year, Americans use billions of plastic shopping bags. Most are used for only a few minutes, but they can last for hundreds of years. Many bags end up in rivers and oceans, where sea turtles and birds can mistake them for food.',
  'Cities that have banned plastic bags have seen fewer bags littering streets and beaches. Shoppers quickly get used to bringing reusable bags. A ban is a simple step that makes a big difference for wildlife.',
  '§Text 2: Think Before You Ban',
  'Plastic bags are a problem, but a ban is not the only answer. Some people reuse plastic bags for trash or for cleaning up after pets. When bags are banned, some shoppers buy thicker plastic bags instead, which use more plastic.',
  'Reusable bags also have a cost. A cotton tote must be used many times before it is better for the environment than plastic bags. Instead of a ban, cities could add a small fee for each bag and build better recycling programs.'],
  q:[['mc','What do the authors of Text 1 and Text 2 <b>agree</b> on?','Plastic bags cause problems.',['Plastic bags should be banned right away.','Cotton bags are always better.','Recycling does not work.'],'Look at the first sentence of each text.','Both say plastic bags are <b>a problem</b>.'],
     ['mc','What does the author of Text 2 suggest <b>instead</b> of a ban?','a small fee for each bag and better recycling',['using more plastic bags','banning all reusable bags','doing nothing at all'],'Look at the end of Text 2.','Text 2 suggests <b>a fee and better recycling</b>.'],
     ['ab','<b>Part A:</b> What is the main claim of Text 1?','The city should ban plastic bags.',['Plastic bags are useful for pets.','Cotton bags cost too much.','Bags should have a fee.'],'<b>Part B:</b> Which sentence from Text 1 best supports this claim?','Cities that have banned plastic bags have seen fewer bags littering streets and beaches.',['Some people reuse plastic bags for trash or for cleaning up after pets.','A cotton tote must be used many times before it is better for the environment than plastic bags.','Plastic bags are a problem, but a ban is not the only answer.'],'Find evidence in Text 1, not Text 2.','Fewer littered bags after bans <b>supports</b> the claim.'],
     ['two','Which <b>two</b> reasons does Text 2 give against a ban?',['Some shoppers buy thicker plastic bags instead.','A cotton tote must be used many times to be better for the environment.'],['Sea turtles mistake bags for food.','Bags last for hundreds of years.','Shoppers quickly get used to reusable bags.'],'Only use Text 2.','Thicker bags and <b>the cost of cotton totes</b>.'],
     ['hl','Tap the sentence in Text 1 that explains how plastic bags harm animals.','Many bags end up in rivers and oceans','Look at the first paragraph of Text 1.','Animals can <b>mistake bags for food</b>.'],
     ['mc','How are the two texts different?','Text 1 supports a ban; Text 2 suggests other solutions.',['Both texts support a ban.','Text 2 says plastic bags are harmless.','Text 1 is a story and Text 2 is a poem.'],'Compare their titles.','Text 1 says <b>ban</b>; Text 2 says <b>think of other ways</b>.']]},
 {t:'How Sea Turtles Find Their Way Home',k:'Article',p:[
  'A loggerhead sea turtle hatches on a sandy beach, scrambles to the water and swims away. It may travel thousands of miles across the ocean for many years. Then, when it is grown, a female often returns to lay her eggs on the same beach where she hatched. How does she find it?',
  'Scientists think the answer is magnetism. Earth acts like a giant magnet, and its magnetic field is a little different at every place on the planet. Each stretch of coastline has its own magnetic "signature."',
  'Researchers believe that young turtles remember the magnetic signature of their home beach. Years later, adult turtles can use that memory like an address to guide them back.',
  'In experiments, scientists placed young turtles in tanks surrounded by special coils that changed the magnetic field. The turtles changed the direction they swam to match the new field, as if they were reading a map.',
  'Understanding how turtles navigate helps people protect them. Bright lights and buildings near beaches can confuse hatchlings, and some scientists worry that changes to beaches could also affect where turtles return. Protecting nesting beaches helps the next generation of turtles find their way.'],
  q:[['mc','What is the main idea of the article?','Sea turtles probably use Earth\'s magnetic field to find their home beaches.',['Sea turtles never leave their beaches.','Bright lights help turtles find food.','Magnets are used to build tanks.'],'What question does the article answer?','Turtles likely use <b>magnetism</b> to find home.'],
     ['mc','What does the author compare a beach\'s magnetic signature to?','an address',['a song','a fingerprint on a window','a ticket'],'Look at paragraph 3.','The memory works <b>like an address</b>.'],
     ['ab','<b>Part A:</b> What did the experiment with coils show?','Turtles change direction when the magnetic field changes.',['Turtles cannot swim in tanks.','Turtles like bright lights.','Turtles only swim at night.'],'<b>Part B:</b> Which sentence supports your answer in Part A?','The turtles changed the direction they swam to match the new field, as if they were reading a map.',['It may travel thousands of miles across the ocean for many years.','Each stretch of coastline has its own magnetic "signature."','Protecting nesting beaches helps the next generation of turtles find their way.'],'Look at paragraph 4.','Turtles <b>changed direction</b> to match the field.'],
     ['two','Which <b>two</b> things can make it harder for turtles?',['Bright lights near beaches.','Changes to nesting beaches.'],['Earth\'s magnetic field.','Remembering their home beach.','Swimming in the ocean.'],'Look at the last paragraph.','<b>Bright lights</b> and <b>changes to beaches</b>.'],
     ['hl','Tap the sentence that tells what female turtles often do when they are grown.','Then, when it is grown, a female often returns','Look at paragraph 1.','Females often <b>return to their home beach</b>.'],
     ['mc','Why does the author use the words "scientists think" and "researchers believe"?','to show that the idea is supported but not completely proven',['to show the author is unsure how to spell','to tell a made-up story','to give an opinion about turtles\' looks'],'Are scientists 100% sure?','These words show the idea is <b>not fully proven</b>.'],
     ['hl','Tap the sentence that explains why every place has a different magnetic field.','Earth acts like a giant magnet','Look at paragraph 2.','Earth\'s field is <b>a little different everywhere</b>.']]},
 {t:'The Lighthouse Keeper\'s Daughter (a story told by Ida)',k:'Story',p:[
  'The storm hit our island just after supper. Father had gone to the mainland for supplies, and the waves were too high for him to row back. That meant the lighthouse was my job.',
  'I was eleven. I had watched Father light the great lamp a thousand times, but I had never done it alone. My hands shook as I climbed the ninety-two steps, counting each one to stay calm.',
  'At the top, the wind howled against the glass. I trimmed the wick, the way Father always did, and struck the match. The first one blew out. So did the second. On the third try, the flame caught, and the lamp blazed to life.',
  'All night I kept watch, winding the clockwork that turned the light and wiping salt from the windows. Around midnight, I saw a fishing boat fighting the waves near the rocks. I held my breath. Slowly, the boat turned toward our beam and found the safe channel.',
  'When Father finally returned at dawn, he found me asleep in the lamp room, the light still burning. He didn\'t say a word. He just wrapped his coat around my shoulders, and I knew he was proud.'],
  q:[['mc','From whose point of view is the story told?','Ida, the narrator, using "I"',['Father, using "I"','a narrator outside the story','the fishing crew'],'Look at the title and the pronouns.','Ida tells the story with <b>"I"</b>.'],
     ['ab','<b>Part A:</b> How does Ida feel when she starts climbing the steps?','nervous',['bored','angry','sleepy'],'<b>Part B:</b> Which sentence supports your answer in Part A?','My hands shook as I climbed the ninety-two steps, counting each one to stay calm.',['When Father finally returned at dawn, he found me asleep in the lamp room, the light still burning.','At the top, the wind howled against the glass.','That meant the lighthouse was my job.'],'Look at paragraph 2.','Shaking hands and counting to stay calm show she is <b>nervous</b>.'],
     ['two','Which <b>two</b> details show that Ida was responsible?',['All night I kept watch, winding the clockwork that turned the light.','I trimmed the wick, the way Father always did, and struck the match.'],['The first one blew out.','The storm hit our island just after supper.','He didn\'t say a word.'],'What jobs did she do well?','She <b>lit the lamp</b> and <b>kept watch all night</b>.'],
     ['hl','Tap the sentence that shows the light helped the fishing boat.','Slowly, the boat turned toward our beam and found the safe channel.','Look at paragraph 4.','The boat <b>found the safe channel</b> by the light.'],
     ['mc','How does Ida know her father is proud?','He wraps his coat around her without a word.',['He tells her she did a good job.','He gives her a medal.','He writes her a letter.'],'Look at the last paragraph.','His <b>gentle action</b> shows pride.'],
     ['mc','Which is the best theme of the story?','Courage means doing what is needed even when you are afraid.',['Storms are never dangerous.','Children should not help their parents.','Lighthouses are easy to run.'],'How did Ida act even though she was scared?','She was afraid but <b>did her job anyway</b>.'],
     ['hl','Tap the sentence that explains why Ida had to light the lamp.','Father had gone to the mainland for supplies','Look at paragraph 1.','Father <b>could not get back</b> because of the waves.']]}
);
