PASS5.push(
 {t:'The Honest Woodcutter (a fable)',p:['A woodcutter dropped his iron axe into a deep river. A water spirit rose up holding a golden axe. "Is this yours?" she asked. "No," said the woodcutter. She showed him a silver axe. "No," he said again. At last she showed him his plain iron axe. "That one is mine!" he cried. Pleased by his honesty, the spirit gave him all three axes.'],
  q:[['What is the lesson of the fable?','Honesty is rewarded.',['Gold is better than iron.','Never go near rivers.','Always ask for more.'],'Why did he get all three axes?','His honesty earned him a reward: <b>honesty is rewarded</b>.'],
     ['Why did the woodcutter say "No" to the golden axe?','It was not his axe.',['He did not like gold.','It was too heavy.','He wanted the silver one.'],'What was his own axe made of?','His axe was iron, so the golden one <b>was not his</b>.'],
     ['What happens at the end?','The spirit gives him all three axes.',['He loses his axe forever.','He keeps only the golden axe.','The spirit takes his axe away.'],'Read the last sentence.','He gets <b>all three axes</b>.']]},
 {t:'Hummingbirds',p:['Hummingbirds are the smallest birds in the world. The bee hummingbird of Cuba is only about two inches long. To stay in the air, hummingbirds beat their wings very fast, which uses a lot of energy. That is why they must eat often, visiting hundreds of flowers each day to drink nectar. At night, some hummingbirds save energy by entering a deep, sleep-like state called torpor.'],
  q:[['Why must hummingbirds eat often?','Flying uses a lot of energy.',['They are always bored.','Nectar is hard to find.','They sleep all day.'],'What does fast wing beating use up?','Fast flying <b>uses lots of energy</b>.'],
     ['What is torpor?','a deep, sleep-like state that saves energy',['a kind of flower','a type of nectar','a fast way of flying'],'Look at the last sentence.','<b>Torpor</b> is a deep, sleep-like state.'],
     ['What is the main idea of the passage?','Hummingbirds are tiny birds that need lots of energy.',['Cuba has many flowers.','All birds sleep at night.','Hummingbirds are the fastest animals.'],'What does every sentence connect to?','It is about how <b>tiny hummingbirds use and save energy</b>.']]},
 {t:'Moving Day (two accounts)',p:['<b>Account 1, from Priya:</b> Today we moved to a new town. I cried when I hugged my best friend goodbye. Our new house smells like paint, and my room echoes because it is empty.','<b>Account 2, from Priya\'s mom:</b> The movers arrived at 7:00 a.m. and finished by noon. We drove 240 miles to our new home. The children\'s new school starts on Monday.'],
  q:[['How are the two accounts different?','Priya shares feelings; her mom shares facts and times.',['Both share only feelings.','Priya gives more numbers.','Her mom describes the smell of paint.'],'Look for feelings and numbers.','Priya tells <b>feelings</b>; Mom tells <b>facts</b>.'],
     ['Which detail appears <b>only</b> in Account 2?','The family drove 240 miles.',['Priya hugged her best friend.','The house smells like paint.','Priya\'s room echoes.'],'Look for numbers.','Only Mom gives <b>240 miles</b>.'],
     ['Why does Priya\'s room echo?','It is empty.',['It is very small.','It is full of furniture.','The windows are open.'],'Read her account.','It echoes <b>because it is empty</b>.']]},
 {t:'Snow Day (a poem)',lines:true,p:['The world went quiet overnight,','a blanket folded, soft and white.','The cars are sleeping, roofs are capped,','the busy street is snowy-wrapped.','','I press my nose against the glass;','no school today! The hours pass','in mittens, sleds and cocoa steam,','a winter day that feels like a dream.'],
  q:[['What does the poet compare the snow to?','a soft white blanket',['a sleeping car','a cup of cocoa','a window'],'Look at line 2.','Snow is <b>a blanket</b>: a metaphor.'],
     ['What is the structure of the poem?','two stanzas of rhyming lines',['one long paragraph','a list of facts','a play with characters'],'Look at the empty line and the line endings.','It has <b>two stanzas</b> with rhyming pairs.'],
     ['How does the speaker feel about the snow day?','happy and excited',['angry','scared','bored'],'"No school today!"','The speaker is <b>delighted</b>.']]}
);
PASS5Q.push(...PASS5.slice(P5d).flatMap((P,k)=>P.q.map((_,qi)=>[P5d+k,qi])));

/* more text-feature pages (RI.5.7): the 7th item field is the page */
const TF5WATER=`<div class="passage tfpage"><h4>The Water Cycle</h4><h5>Evaporation</h5><p>The sun heats water in oceans, lakes and rivers. The water turns into a gas called <b>water vapor</b> and rises into the air.</p><h5>Condensation</h5><p>High in the sky, the vapor cools and turns into tiny drops that form clouds.</p><h5>Precipitation</h5><p>When the drops grow heavy, they fall as rain, snow, sleet or hail.</p><p class="note"><b>Diagram caption:</b> Arrows show water rising from the ocean, forming clouds and falling as rain on the mountains.</p><p class="note"><b>Glossary:</b> <b>water vapor</b>: water in the form of a gas.</p></div>`;
const TF5LC=`<div class="passage tfpage"><h4>The Lewis and Clark Expedition</h4><h5>The Mission</h5><p>In 1804, President Thomas Jefferson sent Meriwether Lewis and William Clark to explore the land of the Louisiana Purchase.</p><h5>The Journey</h5><p>The Corps of Discovery traveled up the Missouri River, crossed the Rocky Mountains and reached the Pacific Ocean in November 1805.</p><div class="note" style="background:#fff6d6;border-radius:12px;padding:8px 12px"><b>Sidebar: Sacagawea</b> A Shoshone woman, Sacagawea, helped as an interpreter. She traveled with her baby son.</div><p class="note"><b>Map key:</b> red line = route west · blue line = route home</p><p class="note"><b>Timeline</b> &nbsp; 1804: leave St. Louis &nbsp;·&nbsp; 1805: reach the Pacific &nbsp;·&nbsp; 1806: return to St. Louis</p></div>`;
BANK3.feat5.push(
 ['features5','Under which heading would you learn how clouds form?','Condensation',['Evaporation','Precipitation','Glossary'],'Clouds form when vapor cools.','The <b>Condensation</b> section explains clouds.',TF5WATER],
 ['features5','Why is <b>water vapor</b> in bold?','It is an important word defined in the glossary.',['It is the title.','It is a mistake.','It is the author\'s name.'],'Bold words are often key terms.','It is a <b>key word</b> explained in the glossary.',TF5WATER],
 ['features5','What does the diagram caption help the reader understand?','what the arrows in the diagram show',['who wrote the page','how to spell precipitation','the date of the last storm'],'A caption explains a picture.','It explains <b>what the arrows show</b>.',TF5WATER],
 ['features5','How are the headings on this page organized?','in the order the steps of the water cycle happen',['in ABC order','from shortest to longest','in random order'],'Evaporation, condensation, precipitation.','They follow the <b>order of the cycle</b>.',TF5WATER],
 ['features5','Which part of the page tells what <b>water vapor</b> means?','the glossary',['the title','the Precipitation section','the diagram caption'],'A glossary defines words.','The <b>glossary</b> defines it.',TF5WATER],
 ['features5','According to the timeline, when did the expedition return to St. Louis?','1806',['1804','1805','1803'],'Look at the timeline.','The timeline says <b>1806</b>.',TF5LC],
 ['features5','What does the <b>map key</b> tell you?','what the red and blue lines on the map stand for',['who led the trip','how long the trip took','what Sacagawea\'s son was named'],'A key explains symbols.','It explains the <b>route lines</b>.',TF5LC],
 ['features5','What extra information does the sidebar give?','who Sacagawea was and how she helped',['the date they left','the name of the President','the length of the Missouri River'],'Look at the box.','It tells about <b>Sacagawea</b>.',TF5LC],
 ['features5','Which two parts of the page tell when the explorers reached the Pacific?','the Journey section and the timeline',['the sidebar and the map key','the title and the sidebar','only the map key'],'Look for 1805.','Both <b>the Journey section and the timeline</b> give 1805.',TF5LC],
 ['features5','Under which heading would you find why Jefferson sent the explorers?','The Mission',['The Journey','Map key','Timeline'],'Why = purpose.','<b>The Mission</b> explains why.',TF5LC]
);

/* ---------- Listening Lagoon: more clips (SL.5.2, SL.5.3) ---------- */
LISTEN5.push(
 {t:'The Underground Railroad (a lesson)',txt:'The Underground Railroad was not underground, and it was not a railroad. It was a secret network of routes and safe houses that helped enslaved people escape to freedom in the Northern states and Canada in the 1800s. People who guided others were called conductors, and the safe houses were called stations. The most famous conductor was Harriet Tubman. After escaping slavery in 1849, she returned to Maryland about thirteen times and led around seventy people to freedom.',
  q:[['What was the Underground Railroad?','a secret network of routes and safe houses that helped people escape slavery',['a train that ran under the ground','a railroad company in Canada','a tunnel under a river'],'Listen to the second sentence.','It was a <b>secret network</b> of routes and safe houses.'],
     ['What were "stations" on the Underground Railroad?','safe houses',['train stops','police stations','schools'],'Listen for the word "stations."','Stations were <b>safe houses</b>.'],
     ['What did Harriet Tubman do after she escaped?','She returned many times and led about seventy people to freedom.',['She built a real railroad.','She moved to Europe.','She became a train driver.'],'Listen to the last sentence.','She went back about 13 times and led <b>about seventy people</b> to freedom.']]},
 {t:'Morning Announcements',txt:'Good morning, Oak Valley Eagles! Here are today\'s announcements. The library will be closed at lunch for a book fair setup, but the book fair opens tomorrow at 8 a.m. in the gym. Fifth graders, your field trip forms are due this Friday, so please bring them to Mr. Chen. Finally, congratulations to our robotics team, who won second place at the county competition on Saturday! Have a great day, Eagles!',
  q:[['When does the book fair open?','tomorrow at 8 a.m.',['today at lunch','this Friday','on Saturday'],'Listen for "opens".','It opens <b>tomorrow at 8 a.m.</b>'],
     ['What must fifth graders do by Friday?','turn in their field trip forms to Mr. Chen',['visit the book fair','join the robotics team','go to the gym at lunch'],'Listen for "due".','Field trip forms are <b>due Friday</b>.'],
     ['What did the robotics team do?','won second place at the county competition',['set up the book fair','closed the library','won first place in the state'],'Listen to the last announcement.','They won <b>second place</b>.']]},
 {t:'Why Leaves Change Color',txt:'In spring and summer, leaves are green because of chlorophyll, the chemical plants use to make food from sunlight. Leaves also contain yellow and orange colors, but the green covers them up. In autumn, the days get shorter and cooler, and trees stop making chlorophyll. As the green fades, the hidden yellow and orange colors finally show. Some trees also make new red and purple colors in the fall.',
  q:[['Why are leaves green in summer?','They are full of chlorophyll.',['They are painted by rain.','They are young.','They reflect the sky.'],'Listen to the first sentence.','<b>Chlorophyll</b> makes them green.'],
     ['What causes trees to stop making chlorophyll?','shorter, cooler days in autumn',['too much rain','hot summer days','strong winds'],'Listen for what happens in autumn.','<b>Shorter, cooler days</b>.'],
     ['According to the speaker, where do yellow and orange colors come from?','They were in the leaves all along, hidden by green.',['Trees make them only in winter.','Sunlight paints them.','They come from the soil.'],'Listen for "hidden".','They were <b>there all along</b>.']]},
 {t:'Interview with a Firefighter',txt:'Reporter: What is the most important thing kids can do for fire safety? Firefighter Reyes: Make an escape plan with your family. Know two ways out of every room, and choose a meeting spot outside. Reporter: How often should families practice? Firefighter Reyes: At least twice a year. And test your smoke alarms every month. A working smoke alarm can give you the extra minutes you need to get out safely.',
  q:[['According to Firefighter Reyes, what is the most important thing kids can do?','make an escape plan with their family',['buy a fire extinguisher','learn to drive a fire truck','stay in their room'],'Listen to her first answer.','<b>Make an escape plan</b>.'],
     ['How often should smoke alarms be tested?','every month',['once a year','every day','never'],'Listen for "test".','Test them <b>every month</b>.'],
     ['Why does Firefighter Reyes say a working smoke alarm matters?','It gives extra minutes to get out safely.',['It puts out the fire.','It calls the fire station.','It is required for school.'],'Listen to the last sentence.','It gives you <b>extra minutes</b>.']]}
);
